\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Pass Valley Yali (ISO 639-3: yac; Glottocode pass1247) is a Trans-New Guinea language of Papua, Indonesia. It is also called Abenaho, North Ngalik or Western Yali. Joshua Project lists about 8,300 speakers. Ethnologue treats it as separate from Ninia Yali (nlk) and Angguruk Yali (yli). Do not confuse it with those varieties.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school materials for Pass Valley Yali were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item General description of the Yali languages. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yali_language}
\item Omniglot page on Yali. \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/yali.htm}
\end{itemize}
The only Yali dictionary found is the Zöllner and Zöllner (2017) Yali (Angguruk)--German dictionary. It covers a different variety, and no URL for it was seen, so it is not listed as a resource.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile. It reports a New Testament in the language (1977--2006). \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/15968}
\item Scripture Earth page for yac, with links to scripture and Global Recordings audio. \url{https://www.scriptureearth.org/00eng.php?iso=yac}
\item Translation-consultant tips page with Pass Valley Yali renderings of biblical idioms. \url{https://tips.translation.bible/tip_language/yac/}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
Audio recordings are reachable through the Scripture Earth page above. No other digital resources were found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Very little is documented. The language is endangered and not known to be taught in schools. Scripture and a few reference pages are the only materials found, so a curriculum would depend on new community-led work.
\end{document}
